Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their possible usage for the task “Which visitor IDs belong to users whose first transaction occurred on a device explicitly labeled as ‘mobile’ on a later date than their first visit?”

• `fullVisitorId`: unique user ID – we will finally return this.  
• `date`: session date in YYYYMMDD – used to compare “first‑visit” vs. “first‑transaction” days.  
• `visitStartTime`: POSIX timestamp – an alternative time field (sometimes handy for ties).  
• `totals.transactions` (INT64) – > 0 means the session contained at least one transaction.  
• `device.isMobile` (BOOL) – TRUE when Google Analytics recognises the device as mobile.  
• `device.deviceCategory` (STRING) – also equals 'mobile' for mobile traffic.  
• Wild‑card table pattern `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*` – lets us read every daily table at once.

Typical conditions we will combine  
• `WHERE totals.transactions > 0` – keep only sessions with transactions.  
• `WHERE device.isMobile = TRUE` (or `device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'`) – keep only mobile sessions.  
• Aggregate per `fullVisitorId` to get:  
  – `MIN(date)` as `first_visit_date` over ALL sessions.  
  – `MIN(date)` as `first_tx_date` over mobile‑transaction sessions.  
• Finally `first_tx_date > first_visit_date`.

Ten step‑by‑step exploration queries
(Each one different, capped with LIMIT 20 for a quick look. Replace / add your own date filters to keep costs tiny while you experiment.)

```sql
-- 1. Peek at a single‑day table to see the columns we need
SELECT
  date,
  fullVisitorId,
  visitNumber,
  totals.transactions,
  device.deviceCategory,
  device.isMobile
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. All sessions (any day) that contain at least one transaction
SELECT
  date,
  fullVisitorId,
  totals.transactions,
  device.deviceCategory
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct device categories that ever record a transaction
SELECT DISTINCT device.deviceCategory
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect only the mobile sessions that have transactions
SELECT
  date,
  fullVisitorId,
  totals.transactions
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
  AND device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'      -- or device.isMobile = TRUE
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. First visit date (any session) per visitor
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. First transaction date per visitor *only on mobile*
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MIN(date) AS first_mobile_tx_date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
  AND device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Combine #5 and #6 to compare dates (no filtering yet)
WITH first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_tx AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_tx_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
    AND device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT
  v.fullVisitorId,
  v.first_visit_date,
  t.first_mobile_tx_date
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_tx t
ON v.fullVisitorId = t.fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Add the key condition: transaction later than first visit
WITH first_visit AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_tx AS (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_tx_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
    AND device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT
  v.fullVisitorId
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_tx t
ON v.fullVisitorId = t.fullVisitorId
WHERE t.first_mobile_tx_date > v.first_visit_date
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Same as #8 but keeping visitStartTime to break same‑day ties
WITH first_visit AS (
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(STRUCT(date, visitStartTime)) AS first_session     -- smallest date then timestamp
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
first_mobile_tx AS (
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(STRUCT(date, visitStartTime)) AS first_mobile_tx
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
    AND device.isMobile = TRUE
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
)
SELECT
  v.fullVisitorId
FROM first_visit v
JOIN first_mobile_tx t
ON v.fullVisitorId = t.fullVisitorId
WHERE (t.first_mobile_tx.date, t.first_mobile_tx.visitStartTime) >
      (v.first_session.date, v.first_session.visitStartTime)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer pattern (remove LIMIT when you need the complete list)
SELECT
  v.fullVisitorId               -- visitor IDs you are after
FROM (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_visit_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
) v
JOIN (
  SELECT fullVisitorId, MIN(date) AS first_mobile_tx_date
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
    AND device.deviceCategory = 'mobile'   -- same as device.isMobile = TRUE
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
) t
ON v.fullVisitorId = t.fullVisitorId
WHERE t.first_mobile_tx_date > v.first_visit_date
LIMIT 20;
```

Run query #10 without the `LIMIT` clause when you are ready for the full list of visitor IDs that satisfy the problem statement.
